{:edb/db-before-t 1648 :edb/db-after-t 1649 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534961 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:49:24.612Z" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186133833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445828 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186133833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445829 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186133834 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445824 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186133834 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445826 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186133835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445825 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186133835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445827 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186133835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445830 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186134020 1050 1080.0 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186134308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131887 13194139534961 17592186045417 false] [17592186134308 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/da/da2895c09bc33f628626257b6d102e74875825df14ba744904e02ffa71552a02.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1+{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{1+{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{1+{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186134308 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/da/da2895c09bc33f628626257b6d102e74875825df14ba744904e02ffa71552a02.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{1+{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{1+{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 false] [17592186134517 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/4f/4fec059132b7bd02cb64f864b6982c0c64a66c22646334ca199fad12097ba435.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186134517 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/4f/4fec059132b7bd02cb64f864b6982c0c64a66c22646334ca199fad12097ba435.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 false] [17592186323953 1074 "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/95/95ebd0a4eb874972b459e53eecf1d57650f63284698fcad967c09620ac01cd99.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=1-x$ intercepts the $x$-axis at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/d0/d048f20f2299722cee598cc1005237627ecdd7643e996b269f26b5a096f01f2f.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{-x+1}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{1}{1+x+y}]}_{0}^{-x+1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{1+x+(1-x)}-(-\\frac{1}{1+x+0}))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-(0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186323953 1074 "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/95/95ebd0a4eb874972b459e53eecf1d57650f63284698fcad967c09620ac01cd99.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=1-x$ intercepts the $x$-axis at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/d0/d048f20f2299722cee598cc1005237627ecdd7643e996b269f26b5a096f01f2f.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{-x+1}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{1}{1+x+y}]}_{0}^{-x+1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{1+x+(1-x)}-(-\\frac{1}{1+x+0}))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-(0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 false] [17592186326875 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/54/54f366679684823341bab818459ce5fc884ebbdd2376e800b781d1b832c4ba64.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,2x\\le y\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2x}^{4}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186326875 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/54/54f366679684823341bab818459ce5fc884ebbdd2376e800b781d1b832c4ba64.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,2x\\le y\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2x}^{4}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 false] [17592186326882 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{x}^{2} & =9-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-2\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/c5/c56c5be4ac88755f1859f295f700be8710d75b16380ac120e6b56a3aa767acbe.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,1+{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 9-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}}^{9-{x}^{2}}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186326882 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{x}^{2} & =9-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-2\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/c5/c56c5be4ac88755f1859f295f700be8710d75b16380ac120e6b56a3aa767acbe.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,1+{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 9-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}}^{9-{x}^{2}}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 false] [17592186326883 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/fc/fcb347767983c38eb12f521ca556dcc5a96705ee4e887fb8d43c42984dfe1a26.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ x\\sqrt{x} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\sqrt{x})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$1\\le x\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/91/91bf7087e5aa0053d24cb45158d48b0bb36fb84b28ebabaeba3c26dc62168dfe.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\frac{1}{x}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1/x}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186326883 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/fc/fcb347767983c38eb12f521ca556dcc5a96705ee4e887fb8d43c42984dfe1a26.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ x\\sqrt{x} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\sqrt{x})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$1\\le x\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/91/91bf7087e5aa0053d24cb45158d48b0bb36fb84b28ebabaeba3c26dc62168dfe.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\frac{1}{x}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1/x}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 false] [17592186351291 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/5d/5d56dfdb75870a251f1eb94f0305a083489ce9e8dc4b9843138cd8ef88da30ab.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4x+6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+y}}{y+1}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{4x+6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+y}}{y+1}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186351291 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/5d/5d56dfdb75870a251f1eb94f0305a083489ce9e8dc4b9843138cd8ef88da30ab.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4x+6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+y}}{y+1}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{4x+6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+y}}{y+1}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 false] [17592186351296 1074 "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/aa/aa635ca5ef3198fea56d684b034de476a72e4ace2a4c51b37c6c59977036eb76.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=e$ intercepts the curve $y={e}^{x}$ at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$1\\le x\\le e.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/b7/b78021a06c9fea100b75c66a619c425345cda3666d84da018895be15a53af06d.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dA={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)]}_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx & ={[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡e}{2}-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186351296 1074 "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/aa/aa635ca5ef3198fea56d684b034de476a72e4ace2a4c51b37c6c59977036eb76.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=e$ intercepts the curve $y={e}^{x}$ at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$1\\le x\\le e.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/b7/b78021a06c9fea100b75c66a619c425345cda3666d84da018895be15a53af06d.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dA={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)]}_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx & ={[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡e}{2}-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 false] [17592186351370 1074 "Notice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the curve with the line $y=0.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/db/db0dd47348fb2f9a97c9e0514f74adae05830de7e03f1aea2886eaa95701200e.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[y]_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186351370 1074 "Notice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the curve with the line $y=0.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/db/db0dd47348fb2f9a97c9e0514f74adae05830de7e03f1aea2886eaa95701200e.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[y]_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 false] [17592186380978 1071 "Calculate ${∬}_{D}x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le x\\}.$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186380978 1071 "Calculate ${∬}_{D}x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le x}.$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 false] [17592186380978 1074 "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le x\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,dA & ={\\in