7592186445822 1078 #edb/ref 17592186445814 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445822 1079 #uuid "b7552f1e-9180-5b58-ac47-de556c1b13e3" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445823 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445791 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445823 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445792 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445823 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445794 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445823 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445799 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445823 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445811 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445823 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445823 1077 "selection" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445823 1078 #edb/ref 17592186445811 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445823 1079 #uuid "ea44a1a7-ed98-5bc5-89ba-bb9dd69cb232" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445824 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445821 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445824 1069 "q-132244" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445824 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445824 1071 "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ and $y=\\sqrt{x},$ then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445824 1072 #uuid "44763ea7-5c70-59d7-96b0-261e9cae9fe6" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445824 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/51/51d1e26c53fd0b3ae404df652eed41cd19604d0358ecb5f4e09727580fc57304.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ x & =2\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(2\\sqrt{x})^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4x \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/05/05740cccba899218f50fc2d9867b968c2b32ca7eaf3ab22a3519387d5756b394.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\frac{1}{2}x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{x/2}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{x/2}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445825 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445819 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445825 1069 "q-132278" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445825 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445825 1071 "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}4x\\,dy\\,dx=$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445825 1072 #uuid "5a3b41b5-2d15-5da0-8405-0eae05fe096e" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445825 1074 "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}4x\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x[\\,y\\,]_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x(x+2-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(4{x}^{2}+8x-4{x}^{3})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(4{x}^{2}+8x-4{x}^{3})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{32}{3}+16-16)-(\\frac{4}{3}+4-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}-\\frac{13}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445823 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445826 1069 "q-132217" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445826 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445826 1071 "![](images/45/45675650564d0e0afa5a1cf1a3f4c786c3443374dee2662e9eb44c10f94089c3.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=4x$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,dA=$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445826 1072 #uuid "65ffb8b6-5f3a-587e-8cef-ea786c6bad11" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445826 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={x}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=-2$ since $x\\ge 0$ in our region. Therefore, $x$-limits are $0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/a7/a7bda76c722de10b988f108c79334227ba2cda82535764ffef76450edaccbb07.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le 4x\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{4x}(x+{y}^{2})\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445822 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445827 1069 "q-81347" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445827 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445827 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy\\,dx=$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445827 1072 #uuid "7dc3d2ae-4ebe-55e5-a13e-304e391443f7" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445827 1074 "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡y}{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡0}{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445820 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445828 1069 "q-81322" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445828 1071 "![](images/16/16ec458c15c9427d3d57c6628438b6aa93960778a46c5df162de5d79b87704c9.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445828 1072 #uuid "c0966490-d3a0-58c5-8173-708ac9df5606" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445828 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/8f/8f18afd79cafdcd0696b3bc25c05fec4109e54cccb084eef49544a4d6599526c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\,dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445818 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445829 1069 "q-132116" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445829 1071 "![](images/e7/e72e6dea415bf05a89518d14dcf932d22cc5774919250638716274d71563c621.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445829 1072 #uuid "caddd05b-4c41-5309-addf-23d1dae6b262" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445829 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/1a/1ab9ae94853a0d161c74aa8d79c47a38f0b0453e0ece320c367098794d5bcb59.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/8}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/8}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\,dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445817 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445830 1069 "q-81349" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445830 1071 "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2-x}2y\\,dy\\,dx.$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445830 1072 #uuid "fd9da944-934f-55d2-b2c0-f2a5f1dc8cbe" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true] [17592186445830 1074 "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2-x}2y\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2-x}2y\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{y}^{2}]_{{x}^{2}}^{2-x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((2-x)^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-4x+4)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-4x+4)\\,dx & =-{[\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-2{x}^{2}+4x]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3}-2+4-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534961 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-042a3167-88c1-58d7-9cb9-db2e59913bc6" 17592186445782 "answer-0bcb67a6-a89e-5cb5-bf50-d01668c64856" 17592186445783 "answer-0d894768-8a15-5793-b9bc-b08a5588612c" 17592186445784 "answer-0e26bbbc-a0dd-5dcf-a436-06546654bda6" 17592186445785 "answer-0e76eacb-ab50-512f-8bc9-2e40397aa3c4" 17592186445786 "answer-0e8c72e1-95ce-588f-83ca-66cb68683c6c" 17592186445787 "answer-143a6761-d36c-525c-8e83-45c2a57cbbb3" 17592186445788 "answer-280f2ce9-93ce-54c2-8464-00d7c0d0608f" 17592186445789 "answer-2aecbf82-cfb7-5a1b-9476-c47c24aef735" 17592186445790 "answer-3da44e10-730a-5257-a521-a8770302d81d" 17592186445791 "answer-4e3a7c89-e6ea-552b-afe0-e00f4ee15120" 17592186445792 "answer-515b98b5-a932-5692-ab25-5bf641f668aa" 17592186445793 "answer-591afc4d-4698-5d08-922d-35f248a7e719" 17592186445794 "answer-678a88ce-8652-55f7-93ad-13e8a6e7dc60" 17592186445795 "answer-7c0ff31b-b685-5181-bcf5-fcb4b5fb5898" 17592186445796 "answer-7ee10c12-4af0-5db5-b53d-68778b815ab0" 17592186445797 "answer-8446a67f-16fd-5996-8bfe-116829320e65" 17592186445798 "answer-905505a9-14b5-5198-8f09-6a3975f0713b" 17592186445799 "answer-9652bca8-4c39-5835-8b2f-41295a63651e" 17592186445800 "answer-96aa0f31-717f-5964-8ba0-b359e5264a03" 17592186445801 "answer-a4b2488a-48f0-57ea-8da8-293b1968eff9" 17592186445802 "answer-a9114ff3-e2ba-5ae9-832c-adc5cedc81f6" 17592186445803 "answer-c1fc5106-7c16-50e1-afac-b55e9efdb392" 17592186445804 "answer-c56a9809-f9cb-5e51-a1c8-7aa478b44cda" 17592